--[[
    PremiumUI — reusable dark grayscale UI library for PBB tools.

    Loads as a module (returns a table, doesn't execute anything on its
    own). One entry point:

        local PremiumUI = loadstring(...)() -- however you load this file
        local window = PremiumUI.new({
            title = "Pokemon Brick Bronze",
            toggleKey = Enum.KeyCode.H, -- optional show/hide hotkey
            -- splash = false,          -- skip the entrance animation, or:
            -- splash = { image = "rbxassetid://...", text = "...", duration = 5 },
        })

    On creation the window plays an entrance animation: the logo pops in
    centre-screen with a tagline beneath it, holds for `duration` seconds,
    then the window opens and the logo shrinks into the title bar's icon
    slot and stays there for the session. The window is hidden until that
    finishes, so nothing flashes behind the splash.

        local mainTab = window:addTab("Auto Finder")
        local section = mainTab:CreateSection("Auto Finder", "Detect and stop on matching encounters")
        -- or target a column explicitly: mainTab.Left:CreateSection(...) / mainTab.Right:CreateSection(...)

        section:CreateToggle("Shiny Whitelist", false, function(state) end)
        section:CreateTextbox("Target Pokemon", "Enter Pokemon name...", function(text) end)
        section:CreateSlider("Encounter Speed", 0, 100, 50, function(value) end)
        section:CreateDropdown("Encounter Mode", {"Legacy", "Modern"}, "Legacy", function(selected) end)
        section:CreateButton("Heal Party", function() end)
        section:CreateTextButton("Reset Stats", function() end)

        local sub = section:CreateSection("Fishing", "Automatically fish for encounters") -- nested sub-section
        sub:CreateToggle("Fish Encounters", false, function(state) end)

    Sections are the panel/subsection system: CreateSection returns another
    object with the exact same CreateToggle/CreateButton/.../CreateSection
    methods, so nesting is just calling CreateSection again on whatever a
    previous CreateSection call returned — there's no separate "subsection"
    type. Controls always live inside a section (there's no direct
    tab-level control placement) — that's the visual design: every category
    is a titled, bordered box, nothing floats loose in a column.

    Every tab has TWO columns (.Left / .Right); Tab:CreateSection(...) is a
    convenience that always targets .Left — call .Left/.Right directly to
    place a section in a specific column.

    Six control types: Toggle, Button, Text Button, Slider, Dropdown,
    Textbox. window:addTab / *:CreateToggle / *:CreateDropdown etc. match
    the naming already used elsewhere in this project's UI code (the old
    UiLibrary), so swapping call sites over is mostly mechanical — the one
    structural change is adding a CreateSection call before the controls
    that used to go directly on a tab.

    window:SetTitle(text), window:SetStatus(text), window:Show(),
    window:Hide(), window:ToggleVisible(), window:Destroy().

    Also on any Container (tab column or section): CreateLabel(text),
    CreateInfoContainer({text=,height=,textColor=,textSize=}) — read-only
    status box, returns {setText=,setColor=} — and CreateButtonRow /
    CreateMoveButtons / CreateTypeSwitcher({types=,default=,height=,callback=},
    returns {setType=,getCurrentType=,setText=}) / CreateActionToggle
    ({text=,callback=}, a stateless toggle-styled button that flashes on
    then springs back off after firing once), all also reachable under
    their old-library lowercase names (addButtonRow / addMoveButtons /
    addInfoContainer / addTypeSwitcher / addActionToggle) for drop-in
    compatibility.

    Old-library method-name aliases on Toggle/Slider/Dropdown returns:
    setState/getState, setValue/getValue, setOption/getOption — all mirror
    set/get. Every stateful control also has setText, matching the old
    library's "override the displayed text directly" escape hatch.

    Config persistence, ported from the old UiLibrary: pass configFolder /
    configGet / configSet to PremiumUI.new(...), then window:SaveConfig(name)
    / :LoadConfig(name) / :DeleteConfig(name) / :GetConfigs(). Uses
    writefile/readfile (or syn.*/fluxus.* equivalents) + JSON, exactly like
    before — no executor support, these just return false.

    Not included yet (the old UiLibrary has this, this doesn't): Divider —
    sections' own borders are the replacement; there's rarely a reason to
    want a bare line inside one now. Ask if you still want it.

    Dark grayscale theme, Inter typography, true rounded window corners,
    and a background grid of diamonds that press inward toward the mouse
    on a damped spring and bounce back out on release.
]]

